\f1\fs22 Kathy\'92s invitation arrived the way corporate disasters always did: politely, with no context, and timed to coincide with being halfway through something important, like applying a patch or pretending she hadn\'92t.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Her tablet chimed once\'97an expensive, tasteful sound that suggested the device had been raised better than she had and would always be more reliable in polite company. The notification banner read:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f2\b\fs18 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>AUDIENCE REQUESTED  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>OFFICE OF THE HIGH ELVIS  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>IMMEDIATE  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>ATTENDEES: HOLY MOMMA, AUNT KATHY, AUNT HAYLEY, SEVEN SAGES CORP  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>ESCORT: PROVIDED  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>NOTES: NO GOSPEL SONGS  \

\f1\b0\fs22 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Aunt Kathy stared at the last line until it stared back.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No Gospel Songs,\'94 she said aloud to the empty kitchen, like she was tasting the words for poison. \'93Righto.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What is it?\'94 Sarah asked, coming in.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy tilted the tablet so she could read it. Sarah\'92s face did something small and unreadable at the last line.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Is that meant to be a joke?\'94 Sarah said, without much curiosity.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Aunt Hayley, leaning against the doorframe with the casual posture of someone whose job was to be underestimated, glanced at the screen and made a small sound in her throat. \'93No gospel music?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s hand rested on her belly. \'93The Grayling warranty said never play gospel to an Elvis. But they\'92re emancipated now. No one\'92s property.\'94 She didn\'92t sound worried, only mildly technical. \'93Why would the High Elvis still care?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Have you ever heard of an Elvis listening to gospel music?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah thought of all the karaoke bars she\'92d visited. \'93No. I can\'92t think of a single time.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy almost laughed, then didn\'92t. \'93If the High Elvis is joking, it\'92s the sort of joke that ends with a riot and a press release.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The sharp, chemical urge for another patch went through her and she ignored it. Not yet. She wasn\'92t going to be the one in the room with the \'93interesting\'94 hands.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley tapped her comms band once, twice. \'93Escort says ten minutes.\'94\
\cb3 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy stood, grabbed her handbag, and checked it the way a diver checks her air before going under. \cb1 Patch packet. A spare patch packet, in case she met someone else\'92s crisis and decided to wear it for them. She hesitated, then added a small bottle of water, because she\'92d learned the hard way that righteous meetings dehydrated you.\
