<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Hayley, Kathy and Sarah sat in the semicircle, the remaining places unoccupied, while the Sages sat to the side in ornate wooden seats that they may or may not have brought themselves. Kleo smiled like an ineffective scarecrow, Chilo scanned the room for betrayal, and Solo plucked at the loose threads of his robes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy could hear the hum of electronics and became intensely aware of her own breathing, and of how stupid it was that breathing could suddenly feel like risky behaviour.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis spoke, his voice quiet yet clear. \'93In the last forty-eight hours,\'94 he said, \'93across multiple venues\'97bars, nightclubs, ice-cream parlours, burger joints\'85 jukeboxes have been sabotaged.\'94 He glanced at the Minister for Grammy Records, who became uncomfortably aware she was expected to finish the thought.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Someone\'92s been replacing a number of records in the jukeboxes with\'85\'94 and here she swallowed hard. \'93Gospel music,\'94 she eventually blurted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Across the room, Chilo tilted his head, as if listening to a distant broadcast no one else could hear. Kleoboulos still hadn\'92t moved. Solo shut his eyes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley broke the silence. \'93So\'85 what?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis\'92s shoulders rose and fell. He looked, for a moment, like a man trapped inside a costume he could no longer take off. \'93So far, every Elvis who\'92s heard a gospel song ends up on their knees, weeping with joy, singing in a perfect four-part harmony no Elvis was ever cloned for. And they renouncing everything that makes Elvi culture so\'85 Elvis.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They\'92re giving up sex and drugs,\'94 the Minister for Health & Prescriptions said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Gratuitous violence towards TV sets and bathroom fixtures is down,\'94 said the Minister for Defence and Karate.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Some say it\'92s sin to shake your hips, for tax sake,\'94 the Minister for the Environment and Miniskirts almost spat.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis said, \'93they\'92re trimming their sideburns.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And there, at the end of the row, one of them already had. \'93Hey! I\'92m a Jailhouse Rock series, through and through,\'94 
\fs20 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 Vince Everett, the Minister for Transport and Road Crew, 
\fs22 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
 defended. \'93I can\'92t even 
\f3\i grow
\f1\i0  sideburns.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Minister for Home Affairs and Divorce, himself wearing conspicuously short sideburns, had been looking down at his hands for some time. Now his lips were moving. No sound at first, then a thread of one\'97a hum, low, sweet and hymnal. Tears lined his face, falling freely. He looked, if anything, relieved, like a man who had finally laid down something heavy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Washed,\'94 he murmured, to no one. \'93I\'92ve been washed clean by\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>No one wanted to hear how he washed. Two Tulsa McLeans seized him before he finished. They perfunctorily gagged him, as if they\'92d rehearsed and been ready, and carried Short Sideburns out. The rest of the cabinet studied the middle distance and pretended, not to heard anything.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The infected are joining a sect calling themselves the Holy Rollers,\'94 the High Elvis said. \'93And gathering in places that used to be ours.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A murmur rippled through the cabinet\'97fear dressed up as decorum.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We have always fought,\'94 the High Elvis said. \'93We fight about lyrics. We fight about hair styles. We fight about rhinestones versus sequins.\'94 His voice sharpened. \'93But this is different. This is \'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Memetic,\'94 Kathy said, before she could stop herself.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Every head turned. She held her smile and wished she hadn\'92t opened her mouth. Chilo\'92s eyes gleamed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis closed his eyes and nodded. \'93Yes,\'94 he said. \'93Their message spreads by song.\'94 He pointed at the Sages, expecting.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleoboulos inclined his head. \'93We have methods.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Methods that do not harm my people?\'94 the High Elvis asked, and the room went colder. \'93They are not to be harmed.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy didn\'92t trust the Sages. Chilo smiled mildly, like a man being asked to do something inconvenient and possibly humiliating with a combine harvester. Solo, beside him, looked like he wanted to be sick.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Holy Momma,\'94 the High Elvis said, quieter, \'93what I have to say almost breaks my heart. It hurts me so bad, I almost don\'92t care about coiffing my own hair.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Beside Kathy, Sarah went still in the particular way Kathy had learned to read: the caught breath, the set jaw, a hand flattening against the belly. Another contraction, and a hard one\'97Braxton Hicks had no business being this sharp this early. More evidence the pregnancy was anything but normal. Kathy catalogued it behind an expressionless face, and pushed down the image the foetal implant kept offering her, of Little Clinton not so much being born as leaving the building before the fans caught on.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The Holy Rollers say you are temptation,\'94 the High Elvis said. \'93They say your child is a test.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A test?\'94 Sarah dismissive. \'93What more than child-bearing in general?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They know about the Dawkins. They know your child is a clone for science, the coming of a false prophet, they say.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah considered this with the mild interest of someone told it might rain later. \'93People have said worse about me before lunch,\'94 she said. \'93He\'92ll manage.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley spoke for the first time, voice level and sharp. \'93Who sabotaged the jukeboxes?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis\'92s cabinet looked away. In a room full of leaders, no one wanted to be the first to say we don\'92t know. Chilo\'92s smile widened by a millimetre, and Kathy caught it, and felt very clearly that at least one of the Sages was enjoying this.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I do not care who did it,\'94 the High Elvis said. \'93Not yet. I care what we do now.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy\'92s mouth went dry. She thought of Sarah\'92s dreams. Of the patterns she saw like language.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis exhaled, long and steady, as if he\'92d been holding his breath for two days. He straightened, cape settling behind him like a verdict. To the Sages, he said, \'93Wise ones. Prepare your ancient methods.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy stared at the High Elvis. She didn\'92t know whether to laugh or vomit. She reached into her bag, felt the reassuring edge of a patch packet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tax it,\'94 she murmured, and applied her patch.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Somewhere in the building, faint as a rumour, someone began to hum.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A gospel tune.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It was, Kathy thought, genuinely lovely, which was the whole horror of it. Every Elvis in the room covered their ears, shut their eyes, and clamped their jaws, as though beauty were a gas leak. Like most songs in that cracked universe, she could only hope they\'92d never agree on the correct words.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She wondered what Chanta would make of it. What he\'92d write.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f3\i\fs24 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Amazing Grease.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>How sweet the taste \'97\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Of burgers charred for thee.\

\f1\i0\fs22 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta, who gave the Elvi every lyric they had, who they called Great Teacher. He would have known exactly what this was. He wasn\'92t here. She missed him, which surprised her.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The words that had united the Elvi had only ever come from Chanta. These came from somewhere else, for a divisive kind of unity.
